{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01MS6f4avGRNpLFBAUAd9Pqg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01QnofCe7mzthXeVj94WfRM1","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/ai-engineering/answers/01-001-how-do-llms-work.md","content":"# How do LLMs work?\n\n**Category:** 01-llm-fundamentals\n**Question #:** 001\n**Source section:** §1 in interview-questions.md\n**Status:** `review`\n**Generated:** ralph-ai-engineering\n\n---\n\n## Framing\n\n### Why this question is asked\nThis is the most common LLM screening question. Interviewers use it to calibrate your baseline: can you explain the core mechanism clearly without reading from a textbook? They want to see whether you understand the transformer architecture at an intuitive level — not just that \"attention is all you need,\" but *why* that matters for generation, scaling, and failure modes.\n\n### Trigger phrases\n- \"How do LLMs work?\"\n- \"Walk me through how a language model generates text.\"\n- \"Explain how GPT-style models work under the hood.\"\n- \"What happens when I send a prompt to an LLM?\"\n\n### What it tests\nAbility to explain the transformer architecture, autoregressive generation, and training objective at a level that bridges theory and production intuition.\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\n### Concept\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) are neural networks trained to predict the next token in a sequence. They learn statistical patterns across massive text corpora using a transformer architecture with self-attention, and at inference time they generate text one token at a time by sampling from a probability distribution over the vocabulary.\n\n### Mechanism\n**Training:** LLMs are trained via next-token prediction (causal language modeling). Given a sequence of tokens, the model learns to predict token N+1 from tokens 1…N. The loss function is cross-entropy over the vocabulary. Pre-training uses vast corpora (trillions of tokens); post-training (instruction tuning, RLHF/DPO) aligns the model to follow instructions and human preferences.\n\n**Architecture (Transformer decoder):**\n1. **Tokenization** — input text is split into subword tokens (e.g., BPE). Each token maps to an embedding vector (e.g., dimension 4096 in LLaMA-3-8B).\n2. **Positional encoding** — positional information is added (RoPE in modern models) so the model knows token order.\n3. **N × Transformer layers** — each layer has:\n   - **Causal self-attention** (masked so token i only attends to tokens ≤ i) — computes Query, Key, Value matrices; attention score = softmax(QKᵀ / √dₖ)V\n   - **Feed-forward network (FFN)** — two linear projections with a non-linearity (SwiGLU in LLaMA); expands then contracts the representation\n   - **Residual connections + Layer Norm** — stabilizes training\n4. **Output projection** — final hidden state → logits over vocabulary (e.g., 128K tokens)\n5. **Sampling** — logits converted to probabilities via softmax; a token is sampled (greedy, top-k, top-p/nucleus, or temperature-scaled)\n\n**Inference (autoregressive decoding):** The model processes the prompt once to build a KV cache (key-value pairs for all attention layers), then generates tokens one at a time. Each new token is appended and the KV cache is extended — so generation is O(n) per token in memory but O(n²) without the cache.\n\n**Scale:** Capability follows the Chinchilla scaling law: performance improves predictably with compute, model size, and training tokens. GPT-4 class models have hundreds of billions of parameters; smaller models like LLaMA-3-8B are useful for on-device or cost-sensitive deployments.\n\n### Example / Tradeoff\n**Concrete example:** When you call `claude-3-5-sonnet` or `gpt-4o` via API with a prompt, the model: (1) tokenizes your prompt with the model's BPE tokenizer, (2) runs a single forward pass to populate the KV cache, then (3) streams tokens back by iteratively sampling from the next-token distribution. With `temperature=0` it's greedy (deterministic); with `temperature=0.7` you get creative variance.\n\n**Key tradeoff:** Autoregressive generation is inherently sequential — you can't parallelize token generation within a single request. This makes latency proportional to output length. vLLM addresses this with PagedAttention for memory efficiency; speculative decoding (running a small draft model then verifying with the large model in parallel) cuts time-to-completion by 2–3× for common outputs.\n\n---\n\n## Verbal script\n\n**Opening (30s):**\n\"At a high level, LLMs are autoregressive transformer models trained to predict the next token. Let me walk through what that means architecturally and how it translates to what you see in production.\"\n\n**Core explanation (2–3 min):**\n\"I'd start with training: the model sees trillions of tokens and learns to predict token N+1 given tokens 1 through N — simple cross-entropy loss, but scaled enormously. The architecture doing this is a transformer decoder. The key building block is causal self-attention: for each token, the model computes a weighted sum over all previous tokens' values, where the weights are learned dot-product similarities. This lets the model flexibly route information — 'which earlier context matters for predicting the next word here?' — and it does this in parallel across all positions during training.\n\nAt inference, the model runs the prompt through all its layers once to populate a KV cache — storing the key and value matrices for every layer — and then generates tokens one at a time. Each new token conditions on everything before it via that cache, so generation is sequential and latency scales with output length.\n\nA concrete example: with a 70B-parameter LLaMA-3 model, each token forward pass requires loading roughly 140GB of weights from GPU HBM. At 2TB/s bandwidth, that's about 70ms per token — which is why LLM inference is memory-bandwidth-bound, not compute-bound.\"\n\n**Tradeoff / production angle (1 min):**\n\"The big production implications: output latency is proportional to length, context length affects memory quadratically (KV cache grows), and larger models are slower but more capable. That's why in production you see model tiering — cheap fast models (GPT-4o-mini, Haiku) for simple tasks, expensive models (GPT-4o, Sonnet) for hard ones. vLLM, TGI, and SGLang address serving efficiency through continuous batching and PagedAttention.\"\n\n**Wrap-up (30s):**\n\"So the core loop is: tokenize → transformer layers with causal self-attention → sample next token → repeat. The magic is that doing this at scale with enough data produces emergent capabilities — reasoning, instruction following, few-shot generalization. Happy to go deeper on attention mechanics, KV cache, or scaling laws.\"\n\n---\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- **Mistake:** Describing LLMs as \"they understand language\" or \"they know facts\" without grounding it in the statistical next-token prediction framing — **Better:** Anchor on \"predicts next token over a vocabulary; knowledge is implicit in the weights from training, not stored symbolically — which is why hallucination happens when the statistical pattern doesn't match ground truth.\"\n- **Mistake:** Skipping the autoregressive inference loop and treating the model like a lookup — **Better:** Explicitly explain that each token is generated sequentially, conditioned on all prior context, and that this makes latency proportional to output length — a key production constraint.\n- **Mistake:** Conflating training and inference architecture — **Better:** Note that during training, self-attention runs in parallel across all positions (efficient with GPUs); at inference, generation is sequential token-by-token (the KV cache is what makes this practical).\n\n---\n\n## Related questions\n\n| Question | Relationship |\n|----------|--------------|\n| [Q2: How do transformers work?](01-002-how-do-transformers-work.md) | Follow-up deep dive into the architecture |\n| [Q9: What is KV cache? How does it help in LLM inference?](01-009-what-is-kv-cache-how-does-it-help-in-llm-inference.md) | Inference efficiency mechanism introduced here |\n| [Q31: How do LLMs generate text? Autoregressive decoding process.](01-031-how-do-llms-generate-text-autoregressive-decoding-process.md) | Decoding specifics (beam search, top-p, etc.) |\n\n---\n\n## One-liner recall\n\n> LLMs are transformer decoders trained on next-token prediction — at inference, they autoregressively sample one token at a time using a KV cache to avoid recomputing attention over the full context.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1033,"cache_read_input_tokens":50472,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1033,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"61a785d6-0651-40b7-9007-759bb84eed6e","uuid":"731b9ced-b41e-4ae0-832c-0e7d2b9a4b5e"}
